# System logs are kept on /data, because the failures that matter here can
# only be read after the reboot.
#
# They used to live in a shared-memory ring: /var/log is a tmpfs, and syslogd
# ran with -C. Everything it held was gone the moment the appliance restarted -
# which is exactly the moment somebody wants to know what happened. An
# out-of-memory kill, a controller that reset the USB bus, a driver that gave
# up: on a headless machine those leave no other trace. The GRBL journal
# survives a reboot and is the reason that gap was visible at all; it covers
# what the bridge saw, and nothing about the system carrying it.
#
# 200 KiB per file and two rotations is at most 600 KiB on the data partition,
# and busybox syslogd does not sync per line, so the cost to a flash device is
# a few kilobytes of appends per hour rather than a write per message.
#
# rc_need is here rather than in the service file because the service file is
# Alpine's. /data has to be mounted and its directories created before syslogd
# can open anything under it, and laserbridge-init is what does the second.
rc_need="laserbridge-init"
SYSLOGD_OPTS="-t -O /data/log/messages -s 200 -b 2"
